From 53a4b07c4551954d25b73fa7876edf07c32ad5b1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 22:48:04 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] VAX8200: configurable VAXBI nodes, twelve MS820 memory
 boards, SHOW BI

A compromise between the standard SIMH VAX 8200/8300 and the real
machine (Ulli, 2026-11-17), after DEC's field service rules for the
82x0/83x0 and the 24-slot VAXBI backplane.

VAXBI nodes
- Every VAXBI device has SET dev NODE=n (0-15 or A-F); a node that an
  enabled device or a memory board uses is refused.  New defaults as on
  a real 8200/8300: DWBUA 0, KA1 1, KA0 2 (the attached processors'
  EEPROMs name node 2 as their console), KDB50 4, DMB32 5, KLESI-B 6,
  DEBNA 7, memory boards from node 15 down.
- The fixed nexus assignments (TR_KA0, TR_KA1, TR_MCTL0/1, TR_UBA) are
  gone.  The KA820 code maps nodes and processors: BIIC register access,
  node private space, RXCD destination and sender node, the IPIR node
  mask, BINID.  VMS shows the primary as CPU 02.
- The DWBUA's Unibus window follows its node; the Unibus devices keep
  their addresses in the canonical window of node 0 (ReadIO/WriteIO
  translate).
- SHOW BI lists the 16 nodes with device, BI device type and details,
  the disabled BI devices and the rule check.
- Rules: at most 3 KLESI-B, 2 DEBNT/DEBNA, 4 storage options (KDB50,
  CIBCA, CIBCI); in the realism mode every DEBNA on a higher node than
  any KLESI-B (SET NODE, BOOT, SET REALISM ENABLED).

Memory
- The MCTL device has twelve boards MCTL0-MCTL11 (MS820-AA 2MB, -BA 4MB,
  -CA 16MB; SIMH-128MB outside the realism mode), each with its type and
  VAXBI node (SET MCTLn TYPE=, NODE=, ENABLED, DISABLED); the memory size
  is the sum of the boards.  SET CPU nM sets the boards from a table:
  4M 2xAA, 8M 2xBA, 16M 4xBA, 32M 2xCA, 48M 3xCA, 64M 4xCA, 128M 8xCA,
  256M 2x and 512M 4x SIMH-128MB.  The realism mode stops SET CPU at
  128M and refuses SIMH-128MB boards.
- The boards get contiguous addresses in board order; each answers with
  BI device type 0001, its start and end address and CSR1 with its size
  and RAM type (bits after NetBSD); SHOW MCTL, SHOW CPU MEMORY.
- VMS uses at most 8 memory boards (V4.7 and V5.5 tested): more are
  allowed, with a warning at SET MCTLn and BOOT.

VAX/tests/vax-diag_test.ini: the diagnostic supervisor attaches the
KA820 at node 2 and the DWBUA at node 0.

Tested: VAX 8200 diagnostics; VMS V4.7 and V5.5 boots from the KDB50
(8200 and 8300, START/CPU, V5.5 SMP); 4M to 128M, mixed boards (54MB),
256M and 512M (V5.5); KLESI-B b1 and b1r, DEBNA V4.7 and V5.5, DMB32
sync line and printer port, VMS from the UDA50 behind the DWBUA at node 0
and node 3.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01SuavtftJEqWTX7fajw6zWU
